      "claim": "Saxitoxin, a polar guanidine-rich neurotoxin, was detected in the brain and medulla oblongata of adult cats after systemic administration, demonstrating that guanidine-containing molecules can achieve CNS access in vivo.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "Cyclocreatine, a guanidine-containing small molecule, showed brain uptake and cognitive improvement in a brain-specific Slc6a8 knockout mouse model, providing direct functional evidence that guanidine-based compounds can cross the BBB.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "AMD3100, a highly basic and charged bicyclam, was detected in CSF after systemic administration, indicating that even strongly cationic molecules can achieve measurable, albeit small, CNS exposure.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "moderate",
  "decision_summary": "The query is a small guanidine-ethyl-azepane. Direct in vivo evidence from guanidine-containing molecules (saxitoxin, cyclocreatine) supports meaningful CNS access, and the azepane ring adds moderate lipophilicity. The main uncertainty is the dicationic character at physiological pH, which may limit passive diffusion, but the positive guanidine precedent outweighs this concern.",
  "evidence_gaps": [
    "No direct brain/plasma or CSF measurement exists for the exact query molecule.",
    "The query is likely dicationic at physiological pH; its passive permeability and P-gp/efflux liability are uncharacterized.",
    "The positive guanidine analogues may rely on transporter-mediated uptake rather than passive diffusion, and the query's transporter interactions are unknown."
